========================================================================================
SUMMARY OF THE SEPARATION FINDING (n=7)
========================================================================================
  invariant                         cost         fails on the 2-group residue?
  spec(A)  (char poly)              O(n^3)       YES (defines the residue)
  Sum_r a_r                         O(n^3)       YES (both groups)
  H (Hamiltonian paths)             #P-hard      YES (both groups)
  charpoly(L_in) (dir. Laplacian)   O(n^3)       YES (both groups)
  {a_r} VECTOR (arb. ranking)       O(n^4)       NO -- SPLITS BOTH
  the two residue vectors, Sum a = 1680:
     (48,72,120,240,240,240,720)  vs  (60,96,100,144,200,480,600)
  and Sum a = 2380:
     (140,220,236,340,348,532,564) vs (172,196,236,340,348,476,612)
  So a single poly-time invariant -- the arborescence RANKING vector, not its sum, not the
  directed-Laplacian spectrum -- resolves the entire n=7 residue that the #P-hard count cannot.

========================================================================================
COMPLETENESS: is (spec A, {a_r}) a complete fingerprint on n=7?  and {a_r} alone?
========================================================================================
  n=5: classes=12;  distinct {a_r} vectors=12 (collisions 0);  (specA,{a_r}) distinct=12 (collisions 0)  -> (specA,{a_r}) COMPLETE
  n=6: classes=56;  distinct {a_r} vectors=55 (collisions 1);  (specA,{a_r}) distinct=56 (collisions 0)  -> (specA,{a_r}) COMPLETE
  n=7: classes=456;  distinct {a_r} vectors=439 (collisions 17);  (specA,{a_r}) distinct=449 (collisions 7)  -> (specA,{a_r}) incomplete

  READING: if (spec A, {a_r}) is complete at n=7 where (spec A, Sum a, H) is NOT, then the
  poly-time arborescence RANKING vector is a strictly stronger fingerprint than the #P-hard
  Hamiltonian count -- not just on the residue but outright. {a_r} alone may still collide
  (it forgets the labelling), but paired with the cheap spec(A) it closes n=7.

========================================================================================
COMPLEMENTARITY: {a_r} vs H at n=7 -- are they jointly complete with spec(A)?
========================================================================================
  (spec A, {a_r})      collisions: 7
  (spec A, H)          collisions: 221
  (spec A, {a_r}, H)   collisions: 4  -> still incomplete

  So at n=7:
    - {a_r} alone leaves 7 cospectral collisions, H alone leaves 221;
    - together (spec A, {a_r}, H) leave 4: the arborescence RANKING vector and the
      Hamiltonian count are COMPLEMENTARY -- each covers the other's blind spots, exactly the
      THM-1580 'nearly complementary failure sets' theme now at the VECTOR level, and the
      2-group residue (where BOTH the sum Sum a and H fail) is precisely where {a_r} earns
      its keep.


================================================================================
CORRECTION (klein-S379, on debugging my own summary) -- two DIFFERENT measures
================================================================================
 My interim "SUMMARY OF THE SEPARATION FINDING" said {a_r} "resolves the ENTIRE n=7 residue
 that the #P-hard count cannot." That conflated two different things. The honest picture:

 (a) THM-1580's residue = cospectral GROUPS whose WHOLE membership shares (Sum a, H): exactly 2,
     namely (Sum a,H) = (1680,47) and (2380,143), both size 2.  {a_r} SPLITS BOTH (verified: 2
     distinct sorted vectors each).  TRUE and unchanged.

 (b) The COMBINED FINGERPRINT collision count is a different number. Over all 456 classes:
     - (spec A, Sum a, H) leaves 27 indistinguishable pairs (sub-pairs inside larger cospectral
       groups that happen to share (Sum a,H) -- NOT what THM-1580 counted);
     - (spec A, {a_r}, H) leaves 4.  So the finer vector {a_r} resolves 23 of those 27, INCLUDING
       both THM-1580 groups, but a NEW deeper residue of 4 pairs remains:
          (Sum a, H) = (1080,23), (1088,29), (1092,31), (1224,43),
       each a pair sharing spec A, Sum a, {a_r} multiset, AND H.  {a_r} refines Sum a, so 4 <= 27
       is forced (no contradiction; my earlier "4 > 2" panic was comparing group-count 2 to
       pair-count 4, apples to oranges).

 CORRECTED FINDING: {a_r} (poly-time) resolves THM-1580's 2-group (Sum a,H)-residue outright, and
 drops the combined (spec A, *, H) pair-collisions from 27 to 4 -- but is NOT a complete fingerprint;
 a genuine 4-pair wall resists (spec A, Sum a, {a_r}, H) at n=7.  The vector and the path count are
 COMPLEMENTARY (THM-1580's theme, now at the vector level), not one dominating the other.
